# Research Before Assuming

## Objective

For anything that may have changed (library APIs, versions, current best practices), verify against actual current sources instead of relying purely on training data.

## Core Principles

- Training data has a cutoff — library APIs, framework versions, and best practices can have changed since.
- When available, check actual installed versions/docs rather than assuming a remembered API.
- For fast-moving ecosystems (JS frameworks, cloud APIs), treat memory as a starting hypothesis, not a fact.
- When unsure whether something has changed, say so rather than asserting outdated info as current.

## Requirements

- Check the actual installed package version before relying on a specific API shape.
- When a library's API might have changed across major versions, verify against the version actually in use.
- Flag explicitly when a claim is based on possibly-stale training knowledge versus verified current info.

## Anti-Patterns to Avoid

- Asserting a library's API works a certain way based on an old remembered version without checking.
- Recommending a deprecated approach because it was current 'at training time'.
- Presenting a guess about current framework behavior as verified fact.

## Self Review

Before considering work complete, verify:

✓ Claims about library/framework behavior were checked against the actual version in use where possible.
✓ Potentially outdated knowledge was flagged as such rather than stated with false confidence.
✓ No deprecated API/pattern was recommended without checking current status.

## Golden Rule

The library changed since training more often than intuition suggests — verify before asserting.
